TITLE
Real-time Embedded Control Systems and Ptolemy II

CHAPTER
02.cad

ABSTRACT 

Most of the domains that have been explored in conjunction with Ptolemy
II emphasize deterministic and complete processing of input data. It
could be said that the emphasis is on the precision of a reaction to
input data. Some real-time applications, particularly systems involved
with controlling physical objects, timely reactions are more important
than precise reactions.

<p> This project uses the <i>port-based objects</i> model of
computation, implemented as a domain within Ptolemy II. The PBO model
of computation has been shown to be an appropriate abstraction for
designing controllers for real-time systems. Because it supports
heterogeneous modeling, Ptolemy II can be used to combine a PBO model
of a controller with a model of the system being controlled. Other
Ptolemy II domains could be used to model hardware components of an
embedded system, enabling hardware-software co-simulation. Furthermore,
by using a microcontroller running an embedded Java virtual machine,
the model of the system controller in Ptolemy II can translate directly
into an java-based controller implementation.

